thread_local! {
    /// How many `binding_owners` walks over a `let`'s right-hand side are open on this thread
    /// (`owners_resting_on`). The walk asks `binding_owners` for every `%name` the right-hand side
    /// reaches, so two `let`s that read each other would walk forever; past `OWNERS_WALK_HOPS` open walks
    /// the walk is cut (`OWNERS_WALK_CUTS`) and the answer is `None`, nobody's binding, the safe side for
    /// the memo and the quiet record. Counted per thread because every scope of one evaluation runs on it
    /// and the walk crosses scopes.
    static OWNERS_WALK_DEPTH: std::cell::Cell<usize> = const { std::cell::Cell::new(0) };
    /// How many times a walk was cut on this thread, at the depth bound or at the distance bound
    /// (`owners_resting_on`). A frame compares the count before and after its own asks: a count that
    /// moved means some binding below it was cut, so the frame answers `None` too; the memo stores
    /// nothing for a frame inside the walk and stores the OUTERMOST frame's answer, the binding the ask
    /// started from, as nobody's binding with a distance past the bound (`memoized_binding_owners`), and
    /// that frame is the one that writes the notice (`EvalContext::note_binding_walk_limit`). So `let a1 =
    /// %a0 ... let a17 = %a16` asked about `%a17` first names `%a17` once, however often `%a17` is read
    /// afterwards, and `a1`, one hop from the document, stays owned for the evaluation: a cut answer is
    /// stored for the frame the ask started from alone, never for a frame inside the cut walk.
    static OWNERS_WALK_CUTS: std::cell::Cell<u64> = const { std::cell::Cell::new(0) };
    /// The longest `let` chain answered since the open frame began, in `let`s (`owners_resting_on`): a
    /// frame's own distance is one more than the longest its right-hand side reaches, read off this cell
    /// after its asks, whether those were walked or answered from the memo (`memoized_binding_owners`
    /// reports a stored distance here). A distance past `OWNERS_WALK_HOPS` cuts the walk as an open depth
    /// past it does, so a chain of more than the bound's `let`s is nobody's binding whichever binding was
    /// asked about first: with `a1` memoized, `a17`'s walk opens sixteen frames, meets the memo and counts
    /// the `let` the memo stands for, so it is cut as a fresh walk is. The memo wrapper sets the cell aside
    /// around each ask it computes and zeroes it after a top-level computed one, so a binding's stored
    /// distance is its own chain's and nothing carries between evaluations on the thread. A memo HIT at the
    /// top level only raises the cell to the stored distance and leaves it there, so between asks the cell
    /// may hold the last hit's distance; that is never read: every reader (`memoized_binding_owners`,
    /// `owners_resting_on`) sets the cell aside with `replace(0)` before it asks and reads it back after.
    static OWNERS_WALK_REACH: std::cell::Cell<usize> = const { std::cell::Cell::new(0) };
}

