    /// Forwarded, as every lookup is: the probe's own queries run with `false`, so this answer is read
    /// only by what the probe forwards to its parent.
    fn in_predicate(&self) -> bool {
        self.parent.in_predicate()
    }

    /// Under a grounding probe, never: that probe runs over ONE ENTRY the real run kept, and the fallback
    /// is a read the real run made from the scope outside the filter, which a probe over the entry cannot
    /// stand in for. Over `Cfg[*][ Mode exists or M[ keys == to_upper(Tags[ Key == 'Env' ].Value) ] !empty
    /// or Ref !exists ]` with the document's `Tags` reading `dev` and the entry's own `Tags` reading
    /// `prod`, the real run read `dev`, failed the key filter and kept the entry by the `Ref` miss alone;
    /// a fallback read under the probe found `prod`, selected the entry's `PROD` key and grounded the
    /// entry through a disjunct the engine itself had failed, so the enumeration passed where it was
    /// refused before. With the in-map miss standing, that disjunct fails under the probe, and a read
    /// that diverges from the real run's can only fail: the safe direction for the grounding probe,
    /// whose failure keeps an entry ungrounded. Under the one-object probe (`takes_the_fallback`), the
    /// parent's answer: that probe's root is the whole map the real `[*]` run reads and its parent is
    /// the scope that run hands on as the resolver, so the fallback read through `fallback_root` is the
    /// real run's read, and a failure that depended on it is the failure the `[*]` reading gives. "Can
    /// only fail" was the unsafe direction there, since a failure lets the filter go on unrefused, and
    /// refusing every filter whose argument the map lacked over-refused the filter whose fallback read
    /// FAILS for real (`Env: dev` beside a map whose keys hold no `DEV`: the `[*]` reading fails the key
    /// filter and passes `empty`, and so does the key spelling now).
    fn reads_beside_the_map(&self) -> bool {
        self.takes_the_fallback && self.parent.reads_beside_the_map()
    }

    /// The parent's root: this scope names itself as its queries' resolver (`query`), so a key filter met
    /// inside a probed predicate asks this scope where the real run asks the scope beside the map, one
    /// level up. Asked only under `takes_the_fallback` (`reads_beside_the_map` refuses the fallback
    /// otherwise, and `FilteredMapFirst` asks for the root only once it is taken).
    fn fallback_root(&mut self) -> Rc<PathAwareValue> {
        self.parent.fallback_root()
    }

